{
  "keystroke_p50": 27.6,
  "keystroke_p99": 41.7,
  "startup_ready": 322,
  "startup_note": "bin/quill from a shell to an editor you can type into, with the 10,062-word document in the profile: chromium process spawn (165 ms of it), profile, window, navigation, fonts and the full render. Median of 4 cold processes, 307-332 ms; the first frame that shows the document is 375 ms. A brand-new profile (no code cache, no storage, empty document - a true first run) is 303 ms to ready, 380 ms to first frame. Inside an already-running browser a page load is 81 ms to ready and 108 ms to the first frame with the document (median of 12).",
  "bar_keystroke": 32.5,
  "bar_note": "iA Writer publishes no latency numbers at all. Bar from third-party measurements of native editors: Sublime Text 32.5 +/- 4.0 ms keyboard-to-photon (Hume, photodiode, 60 Hz panel; TextEdit 33.4, Atom 45.6, VS Code 47.6), and <=5 ms mean / <=16 ms worst app-internal input-to-paint (REFERENCE 5.3, Fatin's Typometer class). The 27.6 / 41.7 ms beside it is built to be comparable to Hume's: 23.64 ms p50 and 37.70 p99 MEASURED on the user's own Hyprland compositor at 60 Hz (3 sessions x 300 keystrokes, worst paced regime - plain writing at the end of the draft), plus 4 ms of panel pixel response CITED from Fatin (the refresh wait is already inside the measurement; Hume's 'keyboard' is a 1 kHz USB emulator, so no keyboard term belongs here). That is the Sublime bracket at the median and the VS Code bracket in the tail, and the tail is the compositor, not the app: Chromium had the frame committed 9.3 ms after the key. On the app-internal bar - the part a builder controls - Quill measures 5.7 ms p50 / 15.0 p99 to a produced frame and 4.8 ms to a committed one, with 99.7% of keystrokes inside one 60 Hz frame. Caveat kept in view: the compositor hop may be one refresh interval smaller on a physical panel (round 1 measured +5.0 ms over commit there, against +17.3 here), which would put the photon figure near 18 ms; no physical panel could be used this round because the user's workspace was in use. Full arithmetic: progress/latency-report.md, section 13.",
  "method": "Per keystroke, from Chrome's own EventTiming trace records (devtools.timeline, unrounded, microseconds): hardware event timestamp to the presentation feedback of the frame carrying that event. Twelve regimes x 300 keystrokes x 3 sessions headless, 7 regimes x 300 x 3 on a real compositor, 10,062-word Markdown document, caret scrolled on screen, 25 warm-up keys discarded. The typist presses real prose: capitals, punctuation, quotes, Enter, Backspace, undo, Ctrl+V paste, select-and-replace and Markdown syntax including opening and closing fenced code blocks - each keystroke labelled by kind and reported separately. Every regime asserts keydowns expected == keydowns in the trace == keydowns seen by an independent in-page rAF/MessageChannel probe, and that all of them have commit and presentation times: true for all 36 headless and all 21 compositor runs. A fresh page per regime makes round 1's double-probe bug unrepresentable. Application cost is measured under prefers-reduced-motion (the app's own caret glide is an animation, and an animation is a frame clock); the headless 60 Hz figure keeps a 1 px composited animation running so headless models a panel. Also measured: CPU throttled 2x/4x, four document sizes up to 55k words, a 2,500-keystroke session with autosave and GC accounting, an idle frame-production control, and true cold starts that spawn a browser process per run. Chromium 151.0.7922.173, i5-13600K, app fingerprint 19b86f97e10b203d, frozen snapshot server. Full method, caveats and raw runs: progress/latency-report.md.",
